course: dictionary
lesson: 2606
title: Depreciation: losing value
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Depreciation is a word about money and value. It means something loses value over time, so it becomes worth less. «A £20,000 new car may be worth £16,000 after one year, losing £4,000.» The car is older and used now, so its value has fallen.
explain: Look for the clues. The sentence says the car is worth less after a year, and it is losing £4,000. So depreciation must mean a fall in value. Check it by swapping your meaning in: «The car's loss of value» still makes sense, so you have it right.
explain: In money maths, depreciation is often a percentage taken off each year. A bike worth 200 that loses 10% a year is worth 180 after one year. Ten per cent of 200 is 20, and 200 minus 20 is 180.
fact: Depreciation means loss of value over time, often because something gets older or is used.
fact: «The car's depreciation» means how much its value has fallen.
fact: «Depreciation of the pound» means the pound is worth less compared with other currencies, so it buys less abroad.
fact: In formal language, depreciation can also mean saying that something is of little worth.
fact: You can spot depreciation in maths by working out a percentage and taking it away from the price.
exercise: A £20,000 car is worth £16,000 after one year. What has happened to its value? | It has doubled | *It has fallen, which is depreciation | It has stayed the same | It has gone up
exercise: «The pound's depreciation means it buys less abroad.» What does depreciation of the pound mean? | *A fall in the pound's value compared with other money | The pound was printed in a new design | A rise in the pound's value | The pound is made of metal
exercise: A bike worth 200 loses 10% of its value in a year. How much is it worth after one year? | 20 | 210 | *180 | 190
test: A shop sells used cars cheaper than new ones because the cars have lost value. What is this loss of value called? | *Depreciation | Generosity | Illumination | Celebration
test: Depreciation of a country's money means its money is worth what, compared with other currencies? | Never printed | More | Exactly the same | *Less
exam: A £500 phone loses 20% of its value in one year. How much is it worth after one year, and what is this loss called? | £400, which is a discount | *£400, which is depreciation | £450, which is inflation | £300, which is a bargain
